/*!
    \qmlmodule RhythmGameQml
    \title RhythmGame QML types
    \brief Game data and reusable behavior for themes.

    Import RhythmGameQml to use these types. Start with the
    \l {qml-api.html}{QML API reference} to choose a component for your screen.
    The \l {../theme_tutorial.html}{theme tutorial} provides installable examples.
*/

/*!
    \page qml-api.html
    \title QML API reference

    Your theme draws the interface. The Standard components handle common game
    behavior, such as browsing songs, starting play and opening results.
    Import \c RhythmGameQml to use them. For a first theme, follow the
    \l {../theme_tutorial.html}{tutorial}; use this reference to look up inputs,
    actions and customization options.

    \section1 Choose a starting component

    Add one of these components to the screen you are building:

    \table
        \header \li Screen \li Component \li What your theme supplies
        \row \li Main menu \li \l StandardMainController \li Menu buttons and artwork
        \row \li Song selection \li \l StandardSelectController \li StandardSelectListView delegates, or a custom wheel
        \row \li Decide \li \l StandardDecideFlow \li Chart or course details
        \row \li Gameplay \li \l StandardGameplayFlow \li Lanes, notes, scores and other visuals
        \row \li Chart or course result \li \l StandardResultInput \li Score displays and Continue/Retry buttons
        \row \li Multiplayer browser \li \l StandardMultiplayerFlow \li Room list and room controls
    \endtable

    Each includes the helpers it needs. For example, StandardGameplayFlow
    already includes StandardGameplayInput. Adding both would handle input
    twice. Each type page lists its included components.

    \section1 Receive screen data

    Declare the matching required property on your screen's root object:

    \table
        \header \li Screen \li Root declaration
        \row \li Decide or gameplay \li \c {required property GameplayContext gameplay}
        \row \li Chart result, including a course stage \li \c {required property ResultContext result}
        \row \li Course summary \li \c {required property CourseResultContext result}
        \row \li Settings \li \c {required property string initialSection}
    \endtable

    The game supplies these values when it creates a screen. Bind gameplay or
    result to the screen's Standard component. Do not construct or destroy the
    supplied contexts or scores yourself.

    GameplayContext follows the current chart and players as a course advances.
    ResultContext keeps the completed chart's metadata and scores fixed.
    CourseResultContext describes the whole course. Read their type pages for
    player counts, missing data and lifetime rules.

    \l {theme-navigation.html}{Screen navigation} explains globalRoot operations,
    settings section names, and opening charts or saved results.

    For shared services, start with the \l {../classRg.html}{Rg singleton}.
    \c Rg.profileList.mainProfile supplies the active profile. During play or
    results, use each player's supplied profile instead. The
    \l {../classgameplay__logic_1_1Player.html}{Player reference} describes live
    scores, columns, note models and timing.

    \section1 Add a feature

    These components can be added without replacing the screen's standard flow:

    \table
        \header \li Task \li Component
        \row \li Connect a song list to a controller \li \l StandardSelectListView
        \row \li Load saved replay events or gauge history \li \l ScoreDetails
        \row \li Show an online rank or open its website \li \l StandardRanking
        \row \li Convert saved note speed into drawing coordinates \li \l StandardGameplayViewport
        \row \li Show a score target or personal best \li \l StandardGameplayTarget
        \row \li Draw BGA and the miss layer \li \l StandardGameplayBga
        \row \li Add Arena ready controls and chat \li \l StandardArenaSelectOverlay
        \row \li Add Arena standings during play \li \l StandardArenaGameplayOverlay
        \row \li Add Arena standings on results \li \l StandardArenaResultOverlay
    \endtable

    Selection already supplies StandardRanking as its \c ranking property and
    StandardSelectPreview as \c preview. Customize those instances instead of
    creating duplicates. Arena overlays belong to the screen; the game does
    not add them automatically.

    \section1 Change behavior

    Start with properties and signals on the complete component. For example,
    StandardGameplayFlow::startReady and StandardGameplayFlow::finishReady let
    an animation delay play or results while the flow still saves scores.

    Action callbacks have two common forms:
    \list
        \li A replacement action handles the entire operation. The standard
            action does not run afterward, and its return value is ignored
            unless the property's documentation says otherwise.
        \li A \c try...Action callback runs first. Return true when it handled
            the request; return false or undefined to use the standard action.
    \endlist

    A request signal asks your theme to act; it does not report success.
    For example, StandardSelectController::moveRequested asks your wheel to
    move. Read each method's return-value description: accepting a request
    does not always mean a screen opened or a load succeeded.

    \section1 Handle inactive screens

    A screen may stay loaded while another screen is shown. The game disables
    inactive screens. Child Items, including the Standard components, inherit
    that state. Standard components stop their own input, timers and audio.

    Your own Shortcut and Connections objects do not inherit an Item's enabled
    state. Bind their enabled properties to your screen where appropriate,
    and stop screen-owned audio and animations while inactive. Hiding an Item
    alone does not disable all of its behavior.

    \section1 Build a custom flow

    Use these smaller pieces when the complete component does not fit:

    \table
        \header \li Task \li Starting point
        \row \li Menu actions without music \li \l StandardMainActions
        \row \li Standard browsing with custom controls \li \l StandardSelectState
        \row \li Custom folder loading and history \li \l StandardSelectSession
        \row \li Open selections with custom browsing \li \l StandardSelectActions
        \row \li Navigation or bound selection keys \li \l StandardSelectNavigation or \l StandardSelectInput
        \row \li Selection function keys \li \l StandardSelectShortcuts
        \row \li Repeat entries for a circular wheel \li \l StandardSelectModelAdapter
        \row \li Shared sort and replay labels \li \l StandardSelectOptions
        \row \li Preview audio or library reload \li \l StandardSelectPreview or \l StandardSelectReload
        \row \li Gameplay input without the standard flow \li \l StandardGameplayInput
        \row \li Retry without an input gesture \li \l StandardChartRetry
        \row \li Detect a scoring hit \li \l StandardGameplayAttemptState
        \row \li Bound lane-key checks \li \l StandardInputKeys
        \row \li Speed and cover input \li \l StandardGameplaySpeedControls
        \row \li Cancel a group of asynchronous requests \li \l PendingReplyGroup
    \endtable

    Replacing StandardGameplayFlow makes your theme responsible for startup,
    score saving, results and course progression. GameplayContext documents
    the operations for that case. Use the complete flow when you only need to
    change the screen's appearance.
*/

/*!
    \page theme-navigation.html
    \title Screen navigation

    The game supplies \c globalRoot to theme screens. Use its named operations
    to open or leave screens. The game manages the screen stack and object
    lifetime. Standard controllers call these operations for their usual actions.

    \section1 Open or leave a screen

    \table
        \header \li Operation \li Effect
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.openSelect()} \li Open song selection.
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.openArenaBrowser()} \li Open the Multiplayer browser.
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.openSettings(section = "")} \li Open Settings, optionally at a named section.
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.returnToPreviousScreen()} \li Close the current screen and reveal the previous one.
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.quitApplication()} \li Quit the game.
    \endtable

    The open operations return the screen, or a falsy value if it could not be
    created. \c globalRoot.currentScreen is the currently presented screen.
    Avoid reaching into the underlying stack or destroying screens yourself.

    A settings screen receives \c initialSection. An empty string means its
    usual first page; \c "keys" requests input configuration. Map these names
    to your own layout and use the first page for unknown names. Calling
    openSettings() while Settings is current keeps its page; passing a nonempty
    section updates initialSection on that screen.

    \section1 Start a chart or course

    \c {globalRoot.openChart(path, players)} and
    \c {globalRoot.openCourse(course, players)} load play and open Decide.
    The \c players argument is optional and defaults to manual play with the
    main profile. Supply one entry for solo or double play, or two for local battle.

    Each player entry has these fields:
    \table
        \header \li Field \li Meaning
        \row \li \c profile \li Required player profile.
        \row \li \c autoplay \li Automatic play; defaults to false.
        \row \li \c replay \li Play a saved recording; defaults to false.
        \row \li \c score \li Full BmsScore or BmsScoreCourse; defaults to null.
    \endtable

    Replay requires a full score with recording data. Autoplay and replay
    cannot be combined. A score supplied without replay uses its saved note
    pattern for G-Battle. Prefer StandardSelectController::openReplay for the
    usual replay action: it chooses a saved run and loads the full score.

    \section1 Open results

    \table
        \header \li Operation \li Input
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.openResult(result)} \li ResultContext from GameplayContext::finishStage().
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.openCourseResult(result)} \li CourseResultContext from GameplayContext::finishCourse().
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.openSavedResult(score, profile, chartData)} \li Full saved chart score and its owner and metadata.
        \row \li \c {globalRoot.openSavedCourseResult(score, profile, course)} \li Full saved course score and its owner and course definition.
    \endtable

    StandardGameplayFlow opens completed results for you. For saved history,
    StandardSelectActions::openResult accepts a cached summary and loads the
    full score first. The two openSaved operations require the full score and
    copy the data needed by the new screen.

    \section1 Replace or retain gameplay

    Custom flows can use \c {globalRoot.openGameplay(gameplay)} to open play
    above the current screen. Arena uses this to retain the screen underneath.

    \c {globalRoot.replaceGameplay(gameplay, previous)} replaces the screen
    holding the previous GameplayContext and all screens above it. Omit
    \c previous to replace the current screen, as Decide does. This operation
    accepts local gameplay only. The replacement is created first, so a failed
    screen load leaves the existing screens available. Both operations return
    the new screen or null on failure.

    The game owns local play for as long as its screens need it. Arena owns
    its own play objects. Themes must not destroy the supplied contexts or
    runners. Use StandardChartRetry for retry behavior and StandardGameplayFlow
    for normal course progression.
*/
